schema: concept-lineage
object:
  title: On-chain attestation as a vouching record
  type: concept-lineage
  short_description: >-
    A digital certificate/proof-record model with three steps: (1) a trusted
    party — teacher, mentor, organization, or system — "vouches for you" by
    making a statement, (2) the statement is written to a blockchain where it
    "cannot be changed, deleted, or faked," (3) anyone can verify it without
    contacting the original issuer. The article frames this as "trust without
    needing a middleman."
  toolkit_usage: >-
    Central mechanism explained in the article as the basis for all four use
    cases it lists (skills, reputation, impact, opportunity access).
  adjacent_meanings:
    - NFT-based credentials (nfts-beyond-art.md treats blockchain diplomas as an NFT use case — same underlying idea, different framing/schema)
    - Verifiable credentials (W3C-style digital credential standards; not named in this article but the closest formal analogue)
  important_distinctions:
    - >-
      An attestation is a claim someone else makes about you, not a
      possession you mint yourself — distinct from an NFT you buy or create,
      even though both live "on-chain."
    - >-
      "Trust without needing a middleman" describes verification, not the
      original vouching step — the attestation is only as trustworthy as the
      issuer who created it; the underlying trust is relocated, not removed.
  tensions:
    - >-
      The article's framing implies attestations are inherently more
      trustworthy than paper certificates, but the trustworthiness of any
      attestation still depends entirely on who is allowed to issue one — a
      point the source text doesn't address.
  risks_of_flattening: >-
    None regarding a cultural tradition; but see accompanying public-use-
    boundary regarding the article's specific, named, vulnerable audience.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/onchain-attestations.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
